\section*{Appendix D: Adaptive Tensor Grid Algorithms}

\subsection*{D.1 Grid Initialization and Resolution Scaling}
The simulation engine operates on a $4096 \times 4096$ adaptive tensor grid.  
Each grid cell stores a multi-physics state vector:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{X}_{i,j} =
\left[
\mathbf{B},\;
\mathbf{u},\;
\rho,\;
g_{\mu\nu},\;
\gamma,\;
\mathcal{F}
\right]_{i,j},
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{B}$ is the magnetic field, $\mathbf{u}$ the plasma velocity, $\rho$ the density,
$g_{\mu\nu}$ the spacetime metric, $\gamma$ the covariance matrix, and $\mathcal{F}$ the fused
telemetry tensor.

Grid resolution is dynamically adjusted according to the residual error:
\begin{equation}
\Delta_{i,j}^{(n)} = 
\left\|
\mathbf{X}_{i,j}^{(n)} - 
\mathbf{X}_{i,j}^{(n-1)}
\right\|.
\end{equation}

Cells with $\Delta_{i,j}^{(n)} > \tau$ undergo refinement.

\subsection*{D.2 Adaptive Refinement Operator}
Refinement is performed using a subdivision operator $\mathcal{R}$:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}(\mathbf{X}_{i,j}) =
\frac{1}{4}
\left(
\mathbf{X}_{i,j} +
\mathbf{X}_{i+1,j} +
\mathbf{X}_{i,j+1} +
\mathbf{X}_{i+1,j+1}
\right),
\end{equation}
which increases local resolution while preserving physical continuity.

\subsection*{D.3 Coarsening Operator}
Regions with low residual error are coarsened using:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{X}_{i,j}) =
\frac{1}{k^2}
\sum_{m=0}^{k-1}
\sum_{n=0}^{k-1}
\mathbf{X}_{i+m,j+n},
\end{equation}
where $k$ is the coarsening factor.  
This reduces computational load while maintaining accuracy.

\subsection*{D.4 Tensor Evolution Equation}
Each grid cell evolves according to:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{X}_{i,j}^{(n+1)} =
\mathbf{X}_{i,j}^{(n)} +
\Delta t \cdot
\mathcal{H}(\mathbf{X}_{i,j}^{(n)}, \mathcal{F}(t)),
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{H}$ is the multi-physics Hamiltonian operator incorporating:
\begin{itemize}
    \item MHD induction dynamics
    \item Dirac--Fock quantum interactions
    \item GR metric evolution
    \item Continuous-variable entanglement updates
    \item Telemetry-driven correction terms
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{D.5 Stability Guarantees}
The adaptive tensor grid ensures:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Local refinement in high-gradient regions
    \item Coarsening in stable equilibrium zones
    \item Conservation of physical invariants
    \item Exascale scalability across decentralized compute nodes
\end{itemize}

This grid architecture enables real-time autonomous correction cycles and stable
multi-physics evolution within the ISSEOM-AI-SEQ framework.